(3918, 2277, 0, '', '', 'I am writing to respectfully express a concern regarding the recent policy requiring training to be conducted exclusively on IITBNF samples by the operators only, and on baseline recipes.\\n\\nWhile standardization for all users is valuable, Ph.D. research relies heavily on specialized recipes and tool protocols that vary significantly between groups. Traditionally, this specialized knowledge is passed down through peer-to-peer mentoring. Under the current policy, students must complete the official training and then undergo a second, unofficial training period to learn group-specific methodologies. This redundancy:\\n1. Extends the timeline for knowledge transfer and project initiation.\\n2. Duplicates effort for both students and lab staff.\\n3. Increases the consumption of valuable lab resources and tool time.\\n\\nWe kindly suggest a review of this policy to allow for more flexibility. We request that the administration consider a more adaptive training model that accounts for diverse research requirements. We look forward to discussing how we can streamline this process to better serve the research advancements.', '2026-02-05 16:35:39', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 4, 2527, NULL, 0),
(3919, 2527, 0, 'Daily Task - Online Updates and others', '', 'Request checking and slot allocation\\n\\nemail communication                                            .', '2026-02-05 16:50:17', 2, '2026-02-06 18:00:00', NULL, 4, 1779, 1779, 1),
(3920, 2527, 82, 'Daily Task  - Tools update', '', 'test runs of Ni & Ti for current, deposition rate, power, beam allignment, substrate shutter issue, vacuum and vacuum time check with EMT person.', '2026-02-05 16:51:04', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 4, 1173, NULL, 1),
(3921, 2527, 0, 'Daily Task -Training', '', 'training meeting 	regarding sputter NMPF                                                             .', '2026-02-05 16:52:06', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 4, 1173, NULL, 1),
(3922, 2277, 0, '', '', 'I am writing to respectfully request a review of the current inactivity duration policy for the Microwriter ML3. Currently, the allowed break period is set at 45 days, which we find to be unnecessarily restrictive when compared to both the technical nature of the tool and the practical requirements of our research cycles.\\n\\nOur request to extend this period to 90 days is based on the following considerations and observations:\\n1. Consistency with Facility Standards: We note that other lithography tools, such as the MJB4, MJB6, and DSA, currently maintain an authorization window of 90 to 100 days. Given that the Microwriter is relatively straightforward in terms of operational complexity, aligning its policy with those of other systems would create a more consistent experience for users.\\n 2. Alignment with Experimental Life Cycles: A typical fabrication and characterization cycle often spans around 60 days. Under the current 45-day threshold, scholars frequently find their authorization has lapsed just as they reach the next lithography stage of a single project.\\n3. Optimization of Resources: Extending this window would reduce the administrative and technical burden on facility operators, who currently must dedicate significant time to re-testing and re-authorizing experienced users whose access has lapsed due to the natural pace of research.\\n\\nWe believe that extending the ML3 authorization window to 90 days would greatly enhance the workflow for Ph.D. scholars and reduce redundant efforts of operators, while maintaining the high safety and tool integrity standards the facility upholds.\\n\\nWe kindly request you to consider this adjustment to ensure a more efficient workflow for all users. Thank you for your time and for your ongoing support of the research community.', '2026-02-05 16:58:33', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 4, 2527, NULL, 0),
(3923, 2527, 197, 'Daily Task -Training', '', 'Sputter Orion	Finalized on sputter Orion SOP                                                                                  .', '2026-02-05 17:21:56', 2, '2026-02-05 10:00:00', NULL, 4, 2546, 2546, 1),
(3924, 2527, 197, 'Daily Task -Training', '', 'Sputter Orion	2nd Hands-on deposited Ti and Au                                                       .', '2026-02-05 17:22:50', 2, '2026-02-05 15:00:00', NULL, 4, 2546, 2546, 1),
(3925, 2527, 330, 'Daily Task -Training', '', 'assisted Mr. Hemant for Target changing in sputter NMPF                                              .', '2026-02-05 17:23:32', 2, '2026-02-05 18:00:00', NULL, 4, 2546, 2546, 1),
(3926, 2276, 0, '', '', 'DC lab should have one extra rack dedicated for dessicators. The dessicators stored by students are taking a lot of space and is causing a lot of clutter.', '2026-02-05 17:32:17', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', 'uploads/3926.jpg', 2, 297, NULL, 0),
(3927, 2277, 354, '', '', 'Following the relocation of the Microwriter ML3 to the Nano Litho Lab, we have observed significant process deviations that affect features of 10 μm or below. While we noted that my recent process complaint was closed successfully, the test patterns used featured 400 μm dimensions, which do not reflect the sensitivities of the smaller features critical to our research.\\n\\nBased on my runs from January 15th–17th, I have identified the following inconsistencies compared to previous standard results:\\n\\n1. Dose Variation: Increase in dose of writing for smaller features of 2 μm with a 2 μm gap. \\n2. Development Time: Development time has increased significantly, from 16 seconds earlier to 24 seconds now.\\n3. Lift-off Issues: Despite the pattern appearing developed, we are seeing persistent metal retention at the edges after Sputter Orion deposition (Ti/Pt 5/30 nm). This occurs even after 4 days in PG remover at room temperature, followed by heating in PG remover at 70 °C, whereas the standard process typically requires only 6–8 hours at room temperature. \\n4. Adhesion Instability: In the same samples where edge retention is high, we are paradoxically seeing metal delamination in the leads (10 μm x 800 μm) and pads (50 μm x 50 μm). This peeling occurs even without sonication; if sonication is applied to aid lift-off, the features break off entirely.\\n\\n(Please see the attached PDF for images and the detailed process flow.)\\n\\nWe use the Microwriter ML3 tool extensively for writing markers and leads with dimensions ranging from 1.5 μm to 10 μm. Earlier, we used to get very consistent results in ML3 for these features. However, the current process shifts are causing delays and wastage of valuable resources. Therefore, we earnestly request that you review the process shifts for these feature dimensions of 10 μm to 2 μm.\\n\\nThank you for your support in resolving these technical hurdles.', '2026-02-05 17:33:43', 1, '2026-02-06 11:00:00', 'uploads/3927.pdf', 4, 928, 2527, 0),
(3928, 1991, 0, '', '', 'Tomorrow morning (05.02.26), help from EMT team is needed to disconnect the existing jacket and furnace controller from the system. ', '2026-02-05 18:04:23', 1, '2026-02-06 10:00:00', 'uploads/3928.jpeg', 1, 2515, 2515, 0),
(3929, 2109, 0, '', '', 'Please remove the desiccators from the location mentioned below, as they create a safety hazard for users.', '2026-02-05 18:43:28', 1, '2026-02-06 15:00:00', 'uploads/3929.jpeg', 3, 2017, 2017, 0),
(3930, 2109, 0, '', '', 'The corridor in front of the 2D Lab, towards the EE Annex, is filled with unused materials and junk. Please see the attached images. Kindly arrange for this corridor to be cleared to ensure easy and safe movement for users.\\n', '2026-02-05 18:48:52', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', 'uploads/3930.jpg', 3, 699, NULL, 0),
(3931, 2109, 0, 'Training on tools', '', '(Re-complaining as the earlier one is closed, and to me the issue is still not resolved)\\nThe existing training and reauthorization framework at IITBNF presents certain procedural inconsistencies and operational inefficiencies, particularly for Ph.D. research users. Training conducted exclusively on IITBNF standard samples does not always translate effectively to research use, as different research groups follow distinct, tool-specific process recipes that are typically transferred through senior researchers. Consequently, users often need to undergo additional training on their own samples after completing the prescribed training, which can lead to extended timelines and additional consumption of time, effort, consumables, and facility resources.\\n\\nWhile the current policy prescribes completion of training within a period of 21 days, strict adherence to uniform training conditions without flexibility in sample type or trainer assignment may limit the effectiveness of knowledge transfer. A more balanced approach could involve initial hands-on training under operators, followed by supervised sessions under authorized users (AUs). In cases where a user is unable to demonstrate sufficient competence during authorization, limited retests may be considered, with repeated unsuccessful attempts reasonably leading to temporary or permanent restriction from tool usage. In such cases, IITBNF may reasonably disclaim liability for user samples used during training, with responsibility resting with the user where expected outcomes are not achieved.\\n\\nFurther, training slot durations may benefit from standardization and capping as determined by the respective Faculty-in-Charge (FIC), with appropriate allowances for tool-specific requirements (for example, a maximum duration of 2.5 hours for sputtering tools and 3 hours for electron beam lithography tools). Additionally, introducing a separate slot category titled “Reauthorization Training/Test” within the booking system would allow users to employ their own samples and established process recipes, thereby reducing procedural friction, minimizing losses for frequent users, and improving overall training effectiveness.\\n\\nIt is also noted that reauthorization practices are presently not uniform across tools within the IITBNF facility. For instance, reauthorization on the Raith system required completion of two hands-on sessions, whereas reauthorization on the ML3 system was conducted solely through a direct test. The absence of standardized reauthorization criteria across tools may lead to variation in user experience and reduced procedural clarity. (also any change in the policy should be communicated via email or put up in the website)\\nIn summary, the current training and reauthorization framework could be further optimized to reduce avoidable wastage of samples, resists, and researcher time, and to ensure that training efforts are closely aligned with the process requirements of users’ research work.', '2026-02-05 19:25:07', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 4, 2527, NULL, 0),
(3932, 2109, 0, 'Baseline', '', 'Please put visible tags on the  baseline resists of litho tools, there are multiple bottles available of the same resist creating confusion over baseline. ', '2026-02-05 19:28:04', 1, '2026-02-06 12:00:00', NULL, 4, 95, 2527, 0),
(3933, 2109, 0, 'Training on tools', '', 'The tool policies for many IITBNF instruments are outdated. In several cases, users are required to complete a specified number of hands-on and eyes-on sessions based on internal or unpublished guidelines. While such requirements may have been discussed in internal or closed meetings, implementing them without making the corresponding policies officially available on the website can lead to discretionary decision-making and tool-to-tool variability.\\n\\nFor example, there is no publicly documented reauthorization policy for the Raith 150 Two system; however, I was required (verbally) to complete two hands-on sessions followed by a test. Similar practices appear to exist across multiple tools. Even if such requirements are applied on a case-by-case basis, it is essential that the concerned SOs clearly communicate the applicable conditions to users through official email communication to ensure transparency and procedural clarity.', '2026-02-05 19:38:53', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 4, 2527, NULL, 0),
(3934, 2149, 0, '', '', 'People, including professors, throw food for dogs near Nanoelectronics Building entry and the region between NanoE and CSRE. Due to this, the dogs in those region chase down anyone nearby. This causes safety issues for us, especially in the night.\\nAs per official IITB guidelines, this is no where near a designated dog feeding spot (https://www.iitb.ac.in/deanpl/pho/fs.pdf). Please raise an official complain regarding this to IITB officials.', '2026-02-05 21:40:43', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 3, 699, NULL, 0),
(3935, 1884, 0, '', '', 'People are giving food regularly near the nanoelectronics building (not a dog feeding area) because of which the area is almost inaccessible. Even though we don\\\'t have food they are aggressively attacking. This happened twice with me within 10 days. I had to run away from that area throwing away whatever I was carrying. The lab surrounding area is unsafe. I had verbally raised this issue earlier. Please raise this issue on behalf of us to higher authorities.', '2026-02-05 21:55:38', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 3, 699, NULL, 0),
(3936, 2149, 0, '', '', 'As per new training policy that has been implemented blanketly across IITBNF without consultation from stakeholders who actually work in the lab and know more about process edge cases than anyone else, i.e., students -  All trainings will be done by operators on standard samples using standard baseline recipes.\\n\\nWhile this will streamline training, it will pose significant challenges for everyone working in the lab, both operators and students. The operators will have to give additional time on training, while students will have to spend additional time getting trained on their specific processes from their seniors. Also, for some tools, the standard recipes involve a very small subset of features of that tool. This results in inadequate training. If the authorisation test is taken comprehensively, there\\\'s a high chance that those trained this way will fail, as they have not seen the complex recipes and edge cases followed by the existing AUs which make use of all the tool features. It is better that they learn about these before authorisation as post authorisation, they will have unrestricted access to the tool and messing with certain features without knowledge and oversight might be harmful for the tool.\\nAlso, some tools don\\\'t require training this way, as they are much simpler or the process steps involved are independent of the recipes.\\n\\nHence, I request you to rethink the policy and have a consultation session with students regarding these policies before implementation.', '2026-02-05 23:46:29', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 4, 2527, NULL, 0),
(3937, 2149, 0, '', '', 'I request you to re-evaluate the inactivity duration (Allowed Break) policy. For some tools, the alloted inactivity duration doesn\\\'t make sense. There is no consistency between the allowed break and the complexity/criticality of the tool.\\n\\nIn my humble opinion, the allowed break should range from 90 days to 180 days. For critical and complicated tools, the allowed break should be a minimum of 90 days. This is a sweet spot between the amount of time one should be able to go without forgetting all process steps, and standard time of fabrication and characterisation run for a device. For non-critical and simpler tools, where one should not forget the process steps even after extended period of inactivity, the allowed break can be extended upto 180 days, depending on the tool. 180 days is the authorisation time limit of the IITBNF labs, as we have to give tests for continued access every 6 months.\\n\\nAlso, preparation of SOP by a student is a mandatory requirement for getting authorised on any tool. The purpose of this is that the AU brings the SOP with them for every slot post authorisation and consults it in case of any doubts in process steps. If the AU always has the SOP on hand, they should be able to use it in case of any doubts post long periods of inactivity. This allows for longer allowed breaks without posing danger to the tool.', '2026-02-06 00:02:05', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 4, 2527, NULL, 0),
(3938, 2149, 0, '', '', 'On the training materials site (http://10.107.103.3/moodle/TRAINING/), the tool training videos of only a handful of tools are available. Even among those, only few videos are working. The rest either lead to dead links or the video doesn\\\'t load.', '2026-02-06 00:22:08', 2, '2026-02-06 12:00:00', NULL, 4, 2527, 2527, 0),
(3939, 297, 156, NULL, NULL, 'New Building  UPS 1  Daily Reading - % Va,	Ts,	Tr,	Ti with log enter in the log sheet \\nFlag the issue if temp is above 40 C.\\nIt needs to be flag to the Team Leads with email and complaint in troubleshooting module\\nCheck if AC are working fine\\nCheck if Exhaust is working fine\\n\\n', '2026-02-06 05:00:02', 0, '0000-00-00 00:00:00', NULL, 2, 692, NULL, 1),
